<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch06_P04_Ex7_square_minus_one</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Statement: \({@bb@}^n - 1\) is a multiple of 8 for every positive integer \(n\).[[if test="is(bb = 9)"]] This is the statement of the exercise above.[[/if]]</p>
<p>(a) Write \({@bb@}^n - 1\) in the form \(m^2 - 1\).</p>
<div>\(m =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) \(m\) is odd, because it is a product of odd numbers. Which of these results, applied to \(m\), finishes the proof?</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</div>
<div>[[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Since \({@bb@} = {@qb@}^2\), \({@bb@}^n = ({@qb@}^2)^n = ({@qb@}^n)^2\). So \({@bb@}^n - 1 = m^2 - 1\) with \(m = {@qb@}^n\).</p>
<p>A product of odd numbers is odd, so \(m\) is odd. For every odd number \(m\), \(m^2 - 1\) is a multiple of 8: writing \(m = 2k + 1\), where \(k\) is an integer, gives \(m^2 - 1 = 4k^2 + 4k = 4k(k + 1)\), and \(k(k + 1)\) is a product of two consecutive integers, so it is even. So \({@bb@}^n - 1\) is a multiple of 8 for every positive integer \(n\).</p>
<p>The other results are true, but they do not apply here: \(m^2 - 1 = (m - 1)(m + 1)\) is a product, not a sum; \(m\) is odd, not even; and three consecutive integers give a factor of 6, not 8.</p>
[[if test="is(bb = 9)"]]<p>Another proof: \(9^n - 1 = (9 - 1)(9^{n-1} + \cdots + 9 + 1)\), which is 8 times an integer.</p>[[/if]]]]></text></generalfeedback>
